      "math_academy_id": "q-184126",
      "sequence_position": 5,
      "problem": "Suppose $n=8s-5,$ where $s\\in Z.$ Complete the following argument showing that $n$ is odd.\n\nThe number $n$ is odd since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =8s-5 \\\\  & =2({{field-1}})+1 \\\\  & ={{field-2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have defined $a=$ {{field-3}}, which is an integer.",
      "worked_solution": "An integer is *odd* if it can be written as $2a+1,$ where $a$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, we complete our argument as follows:\n\nThe number $n$ is odd since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =8s-5 \\\\  & =(8s-6)+1 \\\\  & =2({{4s-3}_{⏟}}_{a})+1 \\\\  & =2a+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have defined $a=4s-3,$ which is an integer.",
